{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And you shall make curtains of goats' hair for a tent over the Tabernacle.</b> First He commanded, 'And you shall make the Tabernacle: ten curtains of twisted linen, blue, purple, and scarlet,' and said, 'The length of each curtain shall be twenty-eight cubits,' and concerning these curtains of goats' hair He said, 'The length of each curtain shall be thirty cubits.' And in the first instance He mentioned ten curtains, while here He mentioned eleven curtains." "<b>And here I am</b> explaining the matter in general terms. Know that the Tabernacle was thirty cubits long, ten cubits wide, and ten cubits high. Its length was thirty cubits, as we find in the boards: 'And you shall make the boards for the Tabernacle: twenty boards for the south side, southward' — and the boards were set vertically, as it says, 'standing acacia-wood' — and they were placed along the length of the Tabernacle, and the width of each board was a cubit and a half, as it is written: 'and a cubit and a half the width of one board.' These boards were twenty for the south side and twenty for the north side — so you have that the width of the boards totals thirty cubits. And its width was ten cubits, from what is written (Exodus 26:22–23): 'And for the hinder part of the Tabernacle westward you shall make six boards,' and it is written (ibid.): 'and two boards you shall make,' and it is written (ibid.): 'and they shall be eight boards in all' for the west side; and this is what is meant by 'and for the hinder part of the Tabernacle' — its Targum is 'and for the ends,' for the west is the end of the east. And just as Scripture calls east 'front' and west 'back,' so too can it call them 'beginning' and 'end.' So you have eight boards that were on the west side, containing twelve cubits according to the reckoning of the board's width being a cubit and a half, and the received tradition of the Rabbis, of blessed memory, regarding the Tabernacle is that its width was ten cubits, and those two extra cubits are what enter into the reckoning of the width of the boards on the north side and the south side, and the thickness of the boards would consume one cubit on each side, so the interior width of the Tabernacle comes out to ten cubits, no more. Now on the east side, where the entrance of the tent was, there were no boards at all, but only four pillars from which the screen was suspended, as it is written (Exodus 26:36): 'And you shall make a screen for the entrance of the tent.' And its height was ten cubits — this is clear from what is written, 'ten cubits the length of a board,' for the height of the Tabernacle is like the height of the boards." "<b>And know</b> that the curtains of goats' hair were placed over the Tabernacle with their length along the width of the Tabernacle. For the length of the curtains is thirty cubits, and when you spread them across the width of the Tabernacle, all the boards of the Tabernacle are covered. Also, the Tabernacle, whose width is ten cubits, and the two walls of the Tabernacle on the north and south, whose height is ten cubits on this side and ten cubits on that side — altogether the thirty cubits of the curtain are consumed by this. If so, when Scripture said first, 'And you shall make the Tabernacle: ten curtains of twisted linen, blue, and purple,' and said, 'The length of each curtain shall be twenty-eight cubits,' it follows that two cubits of the boards remained exposed — one cubit on the north side and one on the south side. Therefore Scripture now comes to say, 'And you shall make curtains of goats' hair for a tent and for a covering over the Tabernacle' — that these curtains of goats' hair should cover the first curtains, which were of twisted linen, blue, and purple. And He mentioned, 'The length of each curtain shall be thirty cubits,' for the length of the first curtains was twenty-eight, and now He commanded concerning these curtains that their length be thirty, so that they would cover those two cubits that remained exposed from the boards. And the Rabbis, of blessed memory, derived from this: the Torah teaches proper conduct here, that a person should be mindful of the beautiful thing — that is, that they should not reach the ground, so that they not become soiled with dust and rain. For the first curtains, which were more precious and esteemed, being of twisted linen, blue, and purple, were not sufficient for this as much as these curtains of goats' hair. Moreover, in the first instance He mentioned ten curtains, and here He mentioned eleven. And this is what is written in this passage: 'and the cubit on this side and the cubit on that side in the excess' — that is, on the north side and on the south side, in the length of the curtains of the tent, it shall hang over, in order to cover the two cubits that remained exposed from the boards by the first curtains, which were twenty-eight cubits. And that extra curtain beyond the first ones, which He mentioned here as eleven, Scripture explained serves as a doubling toward the front of the tent, as it is written: 'And you shall double the sixth curtain' — as if to say, the eleventh curtain, which is the extra one, you shall fold half of the curtain toward the east, at the entrance of the tent, as it is written, 'opposite the front of the tent,' and as the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: it is like a modest bride who places a veil over her face. And half of the curtain he would place toward the west, as it is written, 'it shall hang over the back of the Tabernacle' — and the west is called 'back' as opposed to the east, which is called 'front.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:04:52.893567889Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}